  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "Suppose that \\(P = 2^m\\) and \\(Q = 3^n.\\) Which of the following is equal to \\(12^{mn}\\) for every pair of integers \\((m, n)?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(P^2 Q\\)",
    "B": "\\(P^n Q^m\\)",
    "C": "\\(P^n Q^{2m}\\)",
    "D": "\\(P^{2m} Q^n\\)",
    "E": "\\(P^{2n} Q^m\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Since \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are powers of 2 and 3, rewrite \\(12=2^2\\cdot3\\) using those same bases. The power-of-a-product and power-of-a-power rules give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n12^{mn}\n&=(2^2\\cdot3)^{mn}\\\\\n&=2^{2mn}3^{mn}\\\\\n&=(2^m)^{2n}(3^n)^m\\\\\n&=P^{2n}Q^m.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll bases involved are positive, so these rules remain valid when either integer exponent is zero or negative. Thus the expression is \\(P^{2n}Q^m\\), choice E.",
